roots of their product $p \\cdot q$ is equal to the multiset sum of the roots of $p$ and the roots of $q$, where multiplicities are combined additively. This holds under the condition that $p \\cdot q \\neq 0$, ensuring the product is not the zero polynomial.\n[5] Polynomial.roots_quadratic_eq_pair_iff_of_ne_zero'  (theorem)\n    [Field R] {a b c x1 x2 : R} (ha : a ≠ 0) :\n  (C a * X ^ 2 + C b * X + C c).roots = { x1, x2 } ↔ x1 + x2 = -b / a ∧ x1 * x2 = c / a\n    Let $p(X) = aX^2 + bX + c$ be a quadratic polynomial over a field $R$, with $a \\neq 0$. The elements $x_1, x_2 \\in R$ are the roots of $p(X)$ (counted with multiplicity) if and only if their sum and product satisfy: $$ x_1 + x_2 = -b/a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x_1 x_2 = c/a $$\n[6] Polynomial.Splits.nextCoeff_eq_neg_sum_roots_mul_leadingCoeff  (theorem)\n    (hf : Splits f) : f.nextCoeff = -f.leadingCoeff * f.roots.sum\n    For a split polynomial $ f $, the next coefficient of $ f $ is equal to the negative of the leading coefficient of $ f $ multiplied by the sum of the roots of $ f $, where each root is counted with its multiplicity.\n[7] Polynomial.roots_X_add_C  (theorem)\n    (r : R) : roots (X + C r) = {-r}\n    For any element $ r $ in an integral domain $ R $, the polynomial $ X + r $ has a single root at $ -r $, and its multiset of roots is $ \\{-r\\} $.\n[8] Polynomial.nextCoeff_eq_neg_sum_roots_mul_leadingCoeff_of_splits  (theorem)\n    ∀ {R : Type u_1} [inst : CommRing R] {f : Polynomial R} [inst_1 : IsDomain R],\n  f.Splits → f.nextCoeff = -f.leadingCoeff * f.roots.sum\n    For a polynomial $ f $ over a commutative ring $ R $ that splits into linear factors, the next-to-leading coefficient of $ f $ equals the negative of the leading coefficient multiplied by the sum of its roots (counted with multiplicity). Formally, if $ f $ splits, then $ \\operatorname{nextCoeff}(f) = -\\operatorname{leadingCoeff}(f) \\cdot \\sum \\operatorname{roots}(f) $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"le