$, the system also has exactly two solutions (the origin and another point on the x-axis). The correct set of values should be $\\{8, -8, 28, -28\\}$.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nFind all values of parameter `a` for which the system of equations\n  (x - a)² + y² = 64\n  (|x| - 8)² + (|y| - 15)² = 289\nhas exactly two solutions. The answer is a = 8, -8, 28, or -28.\n-/\ntheorem find_parameter_two_solutions : \n  {a : ℝ | Set.ncard {p : ℝ × ℝ | (p.1 - a)^2 + p.2^2 = 64 ∧ \n                                   (|p.1| - 8)^2 + (|p.2| - 15)^2 = 289} = 2} = \n  {a : ℝ | a = 8 ∨ a = -8 ∨ a = 28 ∨ a = -28} := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 3 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for all real values of the parameter $a$ such that the given system of equations has exactly two distinct solutions $(x, y)$. The first equation describes a circle centered at $(a, 0)$ with radius 8. The second equation describes a curve composed of four circular arcs (from circles of radius 17 centered at $(\\pm 8, \\pm 15)$) restricted to their respective quadrants, meeting at the origin. Geometric analysis shows that the system has exactly two solutions when the circle is tangent to the curve (at $a = \\pm 28$) or when the circle passes through the origin and intersects the curve at exactly one other point (at $a = \\pm 8$). Other values of $a$ yield 0, 3, 4, or 6 solutions. Thus, the complete set of values is $\\{ -28, -8, 8, 28 \\}$.\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean theorem defines the set of parameters $a$ for which the cardinality of the solution set is exactly 2 (`Set.ncard ... = 2`). It asserts that this set is equal to `{a : ℝ | a = 8 ∨ a = -8 ∨ a = 28 ∨ a = -28}`. The equations are formalized correctly using real arithmetic and absolute values.\n\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement accurately reflects the mathematical problem. It correctly encodes the system of equations and the condition of having exactly two solutions. Crucially, it includes all four valid values for $a$ ($\\pm 8$ and $\\pm 28$), correcting the omission found in the previous formalization (which only listed $\\pm 28$). The use of `Set.ncard` is appropriate for counting finite solution sets.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct and exact formalization of the mathematical problem.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The Lean statement matches the mathematical truth. The set of values for $a$ is correctly identified as $\\{ -28, -8, 8, 28 \\}$. The equations and constraints are faithfully represented.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nFind all values of parameter `a` for which the system of equations\n  (x - a)² + y² = 64\n  (|x| - 8)² + (|y| - 15)² = 289\nhas exactly two solutions. The answer is a = 8, -8, 28, or -28.\n-/\ntheorem find_parameter_two_solutions : \n  {a : ℝ | Set.ncard {p : ℝ × ℝ | (p.1 - a)^2 + p.2^2 = 64 ∧ \n                                   (|p.1| - 8)^2 + (|p.2| - 15)^2 = 289} = 2} = \n  {a : ℝ | a = 8 ∨ a = -8 ∨ a = 28 ∨ a = -28} := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "7. [7 points] Find all values of the parameter $a$, for each of which the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}\n(x-a)^{2}+y^{2}=64 \\\\\n(|x|-8)^{2}+(|y|-15)^{2}=289\n\\end{array}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas exactly two solutions.", "num_rounds": 3, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_olympiads", "question_id": "olympiads_3229"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nFor a positive integer $n$, let $I_n=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-|x|\\right)\\cos nx\\ dx$.\n\nFind $I_1+I_2+I_3+I_4$.\n\n[i]1992 University of Fukui entrance exam/Medicine[/i]"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"definite integral over interval Real intervalIntegral\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] intervalIntegral  (definition)\n    (f : ℝ → E) (a b : ℝ) (μ : Measure ℝ) : E\n    The interval integral of a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ from $ a $ to $ b $ with respect to the measure $ \\mu $ is defined as the difference between the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the left-open right-closed interval $ (a, b] $ and the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the interval $ (b, a] $.\n[2] intervalIntegral.integral_cases  (theorem)\n    (f : ℝ → E) (a b) : (∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ) ∈ ({∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ, -∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ} : Set E)\n    For any function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ and real numbers $ a, b $, the interval integral of $ f $ from $ a $ to $ b $ is either the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the unordered left-open right-closed interval $ I(a, b) $ or its additive inverse, i.e., $ \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, d\\mu \\in \\left\\{ \\int_{I(a,b)} f(x) \\, d\\mu, -\\int_{I(a,b)} f(x) \\, d\\mu \\right\\} $.\n[3] intervalIntegral.integral_of_le  (theorem)\n    (h : a ≤ b) : ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ = ∫ x in Ioc a b, f x ∂μ\n    For real numbers $a$ and $b$ with $a \\leq b$, the interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ with respect to the measure $\\mu$ is equal to the Bochner integral of $f$ over the left-open right-closed interval $(a, b]$, i.e., $\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, d\\mu = \\int_{(a, b]} f(x) \\, d\\mu$.\n[4] intervalIntegral.integral_deriv_eq_sub  (theorem)\n    (hderiv : ∀ x ∈ [[a, b]], DifferentiableAt ℝ f x) (hint : IntervalIntegrable (deriv f) volume a b) :\n  ∫ y in a..b, deriv f y = f b - f a\n    If a function $ f $ is differentiable on the interval $[a, b]$ and its derivative is interval integrable on $[a, b]$, then the integral of the derivative from $ a $ to $ b $ equals $ f(b) - f(a) $, i.e., $\\int_a^b f'(y) \\, dy = f(b) - f(a)$.\n[5] intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_le  (theorem)\n    (hab : a ≤ b) (hcont : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b)) (hderiv : ∀ x ∈ Ioo a b, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x)\n  (hint : IntervalIntegrable f' volume a b) : ∫ y in a..b, f' y = f b - f a\n    If $ f $ is continuous on the closed interval $[a, b]$, differentiable on the open interval $(a, b)$, and its derivative $ f' $ is integrable on $[a, b]$, then the integral of $ f' $ from $ a $ to $ b $ satisfies $\\int_a^b f'(y) \\, dy = f(b) - f(a)$.\n[6] intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_tendsto  (theorem)\n    (hab : a < b) {fa fb} (hderiv : ∀ x ∈ Ioo a b, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) (hint : IntervalIntegrable f' volume a b)\n  (ha : Tendsto f (𝓝[>] a) (𝓝 fa)) (hb : Tendsto f (𝓝[<] b) (𝓝 fb)) : ∫ y in a..b, f' y = fb - fa\n    If $ f $ is differentiable on the open interval $ (a, b) $ with derivative $ f' $, $ f' $ is integrable on $ [a, b] $, and $ f $ approaches limits $ f(a) $ and $ f(b) $ at the endpoints, then the interval integral of $ f' $ from $ a $ to $ b $ equals $ f(b) - f(a) $. Formally, $\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx = f(b) - f(a)$.\n[7] intervalIntegral.integral_ofReal  (theorem)\n    {a b : ℝ} {μ : Measure ℝ} {f : ℝ → ℝ} : (∫ x in a..b, (f x : ℂ) ∂μ) = ↑(∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ)\n    For real numbers $a, b$ and a real-valued function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, the interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ in the complex numbers (viewed as a function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{C}$) equals the embedding of the real interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ into the complex numbers. Symbolically, $\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, d\\mu = \\uparrow\\left(\\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, d\\mu\\right)$, where $\\uparrow$ denotes the canonical embedding $\\text{Complex.ofReal}$.\n[8] intervalIntegral.intervalIntegral_eq_integral_uIoc  (theorem)\n    (f : ℝ → E) (a b : ℝ) (μ : Measure ℝ) : ∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ = (if a ≤ b then 1 else -1 : ℝ) • ∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ\n    For any real numbers $a$ and $b$, any function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E$ where $E$ is a Banach space, and any measure $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, the interval integral of $f$ from $a$ to $b$ with respect to $\\mu$ is equal to the product of the sign of the interval (1 if $a \\leq b$, -1 otherwise) and the Bochner integral of $f$ over the unordered interval $(\\min(a, b), \\max(a, b)]$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"absolute value function abs Real\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.nnabs  (definition)\n    : ℝ →*₀ ℝ≥0\n    The function maps a real number $ x $ to its absolute value $ |x| $, considered as a non-negative real number.\n[2] Real.uniformContinuous_abs  (theorem)\n    : UniformContinuous (abs : ℝ → ℝ)\n    The absolute value function $ f(x) = |x| $ defined on the real numbers $ \\mathbb{R} $ is uniformly continuous with respect to the standard metric topology induced by the absolute difference $ |x - y| $.\n[3] Real.nnabs_of_nonneg  (theorem)\n    {x : ℝ} (h : 0 ≤ x) : nnabs x = toNNReal x\n    For any real number $x$ satisfying $x \\geq 0$, the absolute value of $x$ (considered as a non-negative real number) is equal to $x$ itself.\n[4] AbsoluteValue.abs  (definition)\n    : AbsoluteValue S S\n    The absolute value function on a strict ordered ring $ S $, defined as the supremum of an element $ a $ and its additive inverse $ -a $, i.e., $ |a| = a \\sqcup (-a) $, which satisfies non-negativity, multiplicativity, and the triangle inequality.\n[5] EReal.abs_def  (theorem)\n    (x : ℝ) : (x : EReal).abs = ENNReal.ofReal |x|\n    For any real number $ x $, the absolute value of $ x $ in the extended real numbers $ \\overline{\\mathbb{R}} $ is equal to the extended non-negative real number obtained by applying the embedding of real numbers into $ [0, \\infty] $ to the standard absolute value of $ x $, i.e., $ (x : \\overline{\\mathbb{R}}).\\text{abs} = \\text{ENNReal.ofReal}(|x|) $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"cos function Real.cos\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.cos  (definition)\n    (x : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real number $ x $ to the real part of the complex cosine of $ x $.\n[2] Real.Angle.cos  (definition)\n    (θ : Angle) : ℝ\n    The function maps a real angle $\\theta$ (modulo $2\\pi$) to the cosine of $\\theta$, which is a real number. This is well-defined because the cosine function is periodic with period $2\\pi$.\n[3] Real.Angle.cos_coe  (theorem)\n    (x : ℝ) : cos (x : Angle) = Real.cos x\n    For any real number $ x $, the cosine of the angle represented by $ x $ modulo $ 2\\pi $ is equal to the real cosine of $ x $, i.e., $ \\cos(x \\mod 2\\pi) = \\cos(x) $.\n[4] Real.cos_eq_tsum  (theorem)\n    (r : ℝ) : Real.cos r = ∑' n : ℕ, (-1) ^ n * r ^ (2 * n) / ↑(2 * n)!\n    For any real number $ r $, the cosine function satisfies the identity $ \\cos(r) = \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{(-1)^n r^{2n}}{(2n)!} $, where the infinite sum converges to the real cosine of $ r $.\n[5] Real.cos_neg  (theorem)\n    : cos (-x) = cos x\n    For any real number $x$, the cosine of $-x$ is equal to the cosine of $x$, i.e., $\\cos(-x) = \\cos x$.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the problem. Let me write the Lean 4 statement:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nFor a positive integer n, let I_n = ∫_{-π}^{π} (π/2 - |x|) cos(nx) dx.\nFind I_1 + I_2 + I_3 + I_4.\n-/\ntheorem integral_sum_fukui_1992 :\n  let I : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => ∫ x in (-π)..π, (π / 2 - |x|) * Real.cos (n * x)\n  I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem defines I_n for positive integers n as the integral from -π to π of (π/2 - |x|)cos(nx) dx, and asks to compute the sum I₁ + I₂ + I₃ + I₄. The key components are:\n   - Domain: n ∈ ℕ⁺ (positive integers)\n   - Integral bounds: [-π, π]\n   - Integrand: (π/2 - |x|) * cos(nx)\n   - Target: Sum of first four terms (n=1 to 4)\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean code defines:\n   - I : ℕ → ℝ (includes n=0, but problem specifies positive integers)\n   - Integral bounds: (-π)..π (matches)\n   - Integrand: (π/2 - |x|) * Real.cos(n*x) (matches)\n   - Theorem claims I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 0 (asserts a specific value)\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - The domain of I includes n=0 (Lean's ℕ) vs problem's positive integers (n ≥1). This is a minor inconsistency but doesn't affect the specific sum.\n   - The critical issue: The theorem asserts the sum equals 0, but mathematical calculation shows the correct value is 40/9 (≈4.444). This is a fundamental mismatch in the conclusion.\n   - The problem asks to \"Find\" the sum (open question), while the Lean theorem makes a definitive claim about its value. While formalizing as a theorem is acceptable, the claimed value is incorrect.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is incorrect because it asserts an incorrect result (0 instead of 40/9).\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation:\n   - Mismatch 1: The theorem's conclusion (sum = 0) contradicts the correct mathematical result (40/9). This invalidates the formalization.\n   - Mismatch 2: The domain of I includes n=0 (Lean's ℕ) vs problem's positive integers. While not critical here, it's a semantic drift.\n\nResult: Incorrect\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\nLooking at this problem, I need to formalize the integral definition and the sum. The previous formalization was incorrect because it claimed the sum equals 0, when the actual value should be 40/9.\n\nLet me first search for relevant Lean 4 integral notation to ensure proper syntax:\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"definite integral interval notation ∫ x in a..b\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] term∫_In_.._,_∂_  (definition)\n    : Lean.ParserDescr✝\n    The notation $\\int_a^b f(x) \\, d\\mu$ for the integral of a function $f$ over the interval $[a,b]$ with respect to a measure $\\mu$, using the syntax $\\int x \\, in \\, a..b, f(x) \\, \\partial x$.\n[2] term∫_In_.._,_  (definition)\n    : Lean.ParserDescr✝\n    The parser definition for the interval integral notation $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$, specifying the syntax for integrals with a variable, interval bounds, and integrand using the format $\\int x \\text{ in } a..b, f(x)$.\n[3] integral_one  (theorem)\n    : (∫ _ in a..b, (1 : ℝ)) = b - a\n    The integral of the constant function $1$ over the interval $[a, b]$ is equal to $b - a$.\n[4] integral_id  (theorem)\n    : ∫ x in a..b, x = (b ^ 2 - a ^ 2) / 2\n    For real numbers $a$ and $b$, the integral of the identity function $x$ over the interval $[a, b]$ is equal to $\\frac{b^2 - a^2}{2}$, i.e., $\\int_a^b x \\, dx = \\frac{b^2 - a^2}{2}$.\n[5] intervalIntegral.integral_cases  (theorem)\n    (f : ℝ → E) (a b) : (∫ x in a..b, f x ∂μ) ∈ ({∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ, -∫ x in Ι a b, f x ∂μ} : Set E)\n    For any function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ and real numbers $ a, b $, the interval integral of $ f $ from $ a $ to $ b $ is either the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the unordered left-open right-closed interval $ I(a, b) $ or its additive inverse, i.e., $ \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, d\\mu \\in \\left\\{ \\int_{I(a,b)} f(x) \\, d\\mu, -\\int_{I(a,b)} f(x) \\, d\\mu \\right\\} $.\n</tool_response>\nGood, the notation `∫ x in a..b, f x` is correct. Now let me formalize the statement. The problem asks to \"find\" the sum, so I should state what the sum equals. Based on the mathematical calculation (which I won't show here), the correct value is 40/9.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nFor a positive integer n, let I_n = ∫_{-π}^{π} (π/2 - |x|) cos(nx) dx.\nThe sum I₁ + I₂ + I₃ + I₄ equals 40/9.\n-/\ntheorem integral_sum_fukui_1992 :\n  let I : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => ∫ x in (-π)..π, (π / 2 - |x|) * Real.cos (n * x)\n  I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 40 / 9 := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nFor a positive integer n, let I_n = ∫_{-π}^{π} (π/2 - |x|) cos(nx) dx.\nThe sum I1 + I2 + I3 + I4 equals 40/9.\n-/\ntheorem integral_sum_fukui_1992 :\n  let I : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => ∫ x in (-π)..π, (π / 2 - |x|) * Real.cos (n * x)\n  I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 40 / 9 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem defines $I_n = \\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} (\\frac{\\pi}{2}-|x|)\\cos(nx) dx$ for positive integers $n$. It asks for the sum $I_1 + I_2 + I_3 + I_4$. Mathematical calculation shows $I_n = \\frac{4}{n^2}$ for odd $n$ and $0$ for even $n$, yielding a sum of $4 + 0 + \\frac{4}{9} + 0 = \\frac{40}{9}$.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean statement defines `I : ℕ → ℝ` using the correct integral bounds `(-π)..π` and integrand `(π / 2 - |x|) * Real.cos (n * x)`. The theorem asserts `I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 40 / 9`.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean formalization matches the problem's integral definition and summation indices. Crucially, the asserted value `40 / 9` matches the correct mathematical result (unlike the previous version cited in the context which claimed `0`). The domain `ℕ` is a valid superset for the required positive integers in this context.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct formalization.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The integral definition, bounds, summation range, and the specific numerical result `40 / 9` are all mathematically equivalent to the natural language problem.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nFor a positive integer n, let I_n = ∫_{-π}^{π} (π/2 - |x|) cos(nx) dx.\nThe sum I1 + I2 + I3 + I4 equals 40/9.\n-/\ntheorem integral_sum_fukui_1992 :\n  let I : ℕ → ℝ := fun n => ∫ x in (-π)..π, (π / 2 - |x|) * Real.cos (n * x)\n  I 1 + I 2 + I 3 + I 4 = 40 / 9 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "For a positive integer $n$, let $I_n=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-|x|\\right)\\cos nx\\ dx$.\n\nFind $I_1+I_2+I_3+I_4$.\n\n[i]1992 University of Fukui entrance exam/Medicine[/i]", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 4, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": ["unicode subscript digits → ASCII"], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_aops_forum", "question_id": "aops_forum_208056"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n/-- If $p(x)= a_0 + a_1 x + \\cdots + a_m x^m$ is a polynomial with real coefficients $a_i$, then set\n\\[\n\\Gamma(p(x)) = a_0^2 + a_1^2 + \\cdots + a_m^2.\n\\]\nLet $F(x) = 3x^2+7x+2$. Find, with proof, a polynomial $g(x)$ with real coefficients such that\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item[(i)] $g(0)=1$, and\n\\item[(ii)] $\\Gamma(f(x)^n) = \\Gamma(g(x)^n)$\n\\end{enumerate}\nfor every integer $n \\geq 1$.-/"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"polynomial coefficients sum of squares\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Polynomial.coeff_mul_mirror  (theorem)\n    : (p * p.mirror).coeff (p.natDegree + p.natTrailingDegree) = p.sum fun _ => (· ^ 2)\n    For a polynomial $ p $ over a semiring, the coefficient of $ X^{\\deg(p) + \\operatorname{tdeg}(p)} $ in the product $ p \\cdot \\operatorname{mirror}(p) $ is equal to the sum of the squares of all coefficients of $ p $, where $ \\deg(p) $ denotes the natural degree of $ p $ and $ \\operatorname{tdeg}(p) $ denotes its trailing degree.\n[2] Poly.sumsq  (definition)\n    : List (Poly α) → Poly α\n    The function takes a list of integer polynomials in variables indexed by $\\alpha$ and returns the sum of their squares. For an empty list, it returns the zero polynomial. For a non-empty list $[p, ps]$, it computes $p \\cdot p + \\text{sumsq}(ps)$, where $\\cdot$ denotes polynomial multiplication.\n[3] Poly.sumsq_nonneg  (theorem)\n    (x : α → ℕ) : ∀ l, 0 ≤ sumsq l x\n    For any assignment $x : \\alpha \\to \\mathbb{N}$ of natural numbers to variables indexed by $\\alpha$, the evaluation of the sum of squares of a list of integer polynomials at $x$ yields a non-negative integer, i.e., $\\text{sumsq}(l)(x) \\geq 0$ for all lists $l$ of polynomials in $\\text{Poly}\\, \\alpha$.\n[4] Poly.sumsq_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    (x) : ∀ l, sumsq l x = 0 ↔ l.Forall fun a : Poly α => a x = 0\n    For any list $ l $ of integer polynomials in variables indexed by $ \\alpha $ and for any evaluation point $ x $, the sum of the squares of the polynomials in $ l $ evaluated at $ x $ is zero if and only if each polynomial in $ l $ is zero at $ x $.\n[5] Polynomial.coeff_sum  (theorem)\n    [Semiring S] (n : ℕ) (f : ℕ → R → S[X]) : coeff (p.sum f) n = p.sum fun a b => coeff (f a b) n\n    For any polynomial $ p $ over a semiring $ R $, any additive commutative monoid $ S $, and any function $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to R \\to S[X] $, the coefficient of degree $ n $ in the sum $ \\sum f(m)(a_m) $ (where $ a_m $ is the coefficient of $ X^m $ in $ p $) is equal to the sum of the coefficients of degree $ n $ in each $ f(m)(a_m) $. In symbols, $ \\operatorname{coeff}(\\sum f(m)(a_m), n) = \\sum \\operatorname{coeff}(f(m)(a_m), n) $.\n[6] Polynomial.coeff_list_sum  (theorem)\n    (l : List R[X]) (n : ℕ) : l.sum.coeff n = (l.map (lcoeff R n)).sum\n    For any list of polynomials $ l $ over a semiring $ R $ and any natural number $ n $, the coefficient of $ X^n $ in the sum of the polynomials in $ l $ is equal to the sum of the coefficients of $ X^n $ in each individual polynomial of $ l $. Formally, $ (\\sum l).\\text{coeff}(n) = \\sum_{p \\in l} p.\\text{coeff}(n) $.\n[7] Polynomial.finset_sum_coeff  (theorem)\n    {ι : Type*} (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → R[X]) (n : ℕ) : coeff (∑ b ∈ s, f b) n = ∑ b ∈ s, coeff (f b) n\n    For any finite set $ S $ of polynomials in $ R[X] $ and any natural number $ n $, the coefficient of $ X^n $ in the sum of the polynomials in $ S $ is equal to the sum of the coefficients of $ X^n $ in each polynomial of $ S $.\n[8] MvPolynomial.coeff_sum  (theorem)\n    {X : Type*} (s : Finset X) (f : X → MvPolynomial σ R) (m : σ →₀ ℕ) : coeff m (∑ x ∈ s, f x) = ∑ x ∈ s, coeff m (f x)\n    For any commutative semiring $ R $, type $ \\sigma $ indexing variables, finite set $ s $ of type $ X $, and function $ f : X \\to R[\\sigma] $, the coefficient of a monomial $ m : \\sigma \\to_0 \\mathbb{N} $ in the sum $ \\sum_{x \\in s} f(x) $ is equal to the sum of the coefficients of $ m $ in each $ f(x) $, i.e., $ \\operatorname{coeff}_m\\left(\\sum_{x \\in s} f(x)\\right) = \\sum_{x \\in s} \\operatorname{coeff}_m(f(x)) $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Polynomial mirror definition\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Polynomial.mirror  (definition)\n    {R : Type u_1} → [inst : Semiring R] → Polynomial R → Polynomial R\n    The mirror of a polynomial $ p $ is defined as the reversed polynomial multiplied by $ X $ raised to the trailing degree of $ p $. This operation reverses the coefficients of $ p $ up to its natural degree and scales the result by $ X $ raised to the minimum exponent of its non-zero coefficients (with $ 0 $ for the zero polynomial).\n[2] Polynomial.mirror_monomial  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) (a : R) : (monomial n a).mirror = monomial n a\n    For any natural number $n$ and element $a$ in a semiring $R$, the mirror operation applied to the monomial polynomial $aX^n$ yields the same monomial, i.e., $\\text{mirror}(aX^n) = aX^n$.\n[3] Polynomial.reverse  (definition)\n    (f : R[X]) : R[X]\n    The function maps a polynomial $ f $ to the polynomial obtained by reversing its coefficients up to its natural degree, leaving coefficients beyond that degree unchanged.\n[4] Polynomial.mirror_mirror  (theorem)\n    : p.mirror.mirror = p\n    For any polynomial $ p $, applying the mirror operation twice results in the original polynomial $ p $, i.e., $ \\text{mirror}(\\text{mirror}(p)) = p $.\n[5] Polynomial.mirror_X  (theorem)\n    : X.mirror = (X : R[X])\n    The mirror operation applied to the indeterminate $ X $ in the polynomial ring $ R[X] $ yields $ X $ itself, i.e., $ \\text{mirror}(X) = X $. This follows because reversing the coefficients of $ X $ (which has a single non-zero coefficient at degree 1) and scaling by $ X^{\\text{trailing degree}} = X^1 $ leaves the polynomial unchanged.\n[6] Polynomial.mirror_C  (theorem)\n    (a : R) : (C a).mirror = C a\n    For any element $ a $ in a semiring $ R $, the mirror of the constant polynomial $ a $ in $ R[X] $ is equal to $ a $ itself, i.e., $ \\text{mirror}(a) = a $.\n[7] Polynomial.trinomial_mirror  (theorem)\n    (hkm : k < m) (hmn : m < n) (hu : u ≠ 0) (hw : w ≠ 0) :\n  (trinomial k m n u v w).mirror = trinomial k (n - m + k) n w v u\n    For a trinomial polynomial $uX^k + v X^m + w X^n$ over a semiring with $k < m < n$ and $u,w \\neq 0$, its mirror is the trinomial $wX^k + v X^{n - m + k} + u X^n$. This follows from reversing the coefficient sequence and scaling by $X$ raised to the trailing degree, which swaps the leading and trailing terms while adjusting the middle exponent to $n - m + k$.\n[8] Polynomial.reflect  (definition)\n    (N : ℕ) : R[X] → R[X]\n    Given a polynomial $ f $ and a natural number $ N $, this function returns the polynomial obtained by reversing the coefficients of $ f $ for degrees $ \\leq N $ using the index reversal map $ \\text{revAt}(N) $, which sends $ i \\leq N $ to $ N - i $, while leaving coefficients at degrees $ > N $ unchanged.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"sum of squares of coefficients polynomial\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Polynomial.coeff_mul_mirror  (theorem)\n    : (p * p.mirror).coeff (p.natDegree + p.natTrailingDegree) = p.sum fun _ => (· ^ 2)\n    For a polynomial $ p $ over a semiring, the coefficient of $ X^{\\deg(p) + \\operatorname{tdeg}(p)} $ in the product $ p \\cdot \\operatorname{mirror}(p) $ is equal to the sum of the squares of all coefficients of $ p $, where $ \\deg(p) $ denotes the natural degree of $ p $ and $ \\operatorname{tdeg}(p) $ denotes its trailing degree.\n[2] Poly.sumsq  (definition)\n    : List (Poly α) → Poly α\n    The function takes a list of integer polynomials in variables indexed by $\\alpha$ and returns the sum of their squares. For an empty list, it returns the zero polynomial. For a non-empty list $[p, ps]$, it computes $p \\cdot p + \\text{sumsq}(ps)$, where $\\cdot$ denotes polynomial multiplication.\n[3] Poly.sumsq_nonneg  (theorem)\n    (x : α → ℕ) : ∀ l, 0 ≤ sumsq l x\n    For any assignment $x : \\alpha \\to \\mathbb{N}$ of natural numbers to variables indexed by $\\alpha$, the evaluation of the sum of squares of a list of integer polynomials at $x$ yields a non-negative integer, i.e., $\\text{sumsq}(l)(x) \\geq 0$ for all lists $l$ of polynomials in $\\text{Poly}\\, \\alpha$.\n[4] hasSum_sq_fourierCoeffOn  (theorem)\n    {a b : ℝ} {f : ℝ → ℂ} (hab : a < b) (hL2 : MemLp f 2 (volume.restrict (Ioc a b))) :\n  HasSum (fun i => ‖fourierCoeffOn hab f i‖ ^ 2) ((b - a)⁻¹ • ∫ x in a..b, ‖f x‖ ^ 2)\n    For real numbers $a < b$ and a function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{C}$ in $L^2$ on $(a, b]$, the sum of the squares of the Fourier coefficients of $f$ over $(a, b]$ equals $\\frac{1}{b - a}$ times the integral of $|f(x)|^2$ over $[a, b]$. Specifically, $\\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\left\\|\\int_{t \\in (a,b]} e^{-i \\frac{2\\pi n}{b-a}(t - a)} f(t)\\, dt\\right\\|^2 = \\frac{1}{b - a} \\int_{a}^{b} |f(x)|^2 \\, dx$.\n[5] Poly.sumsq_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    (x) : ∀ l, sumsq l x = 0 ↔ l.Forall fun a : Poly α => a x = 0\n    For any list $ l $ of integer polynomials in variables indexed by $ \\alpha $ and for any evaluation point $ x $, the sum of the squares of the polynomials in $ l $ evaluated at $ x $ is zero if and only if each polynomial in $ l $ is zero at $ x $.\n[6] tsum_sq_fourierCoeffOn  (theorem)\n    {a b : ℝ} {f : ℝ → ℂ} (hab : a < b) (hL2 : MemLp f 2 (volume.restrict (Ioc a b))) :\n  ∑' (i : ℤ), ‖fourierCoeffOn hab f i‖ ^ 2 = (b - a)⁻¹ • ∫ x in a..b, ‖f x‖ ^ 2\n    For real numbers $a < b$ and a function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{C}$ that is square-integrable on $(a, b]$, the sum of the squared moduli of its Fourier coefficients equals $\\frac{1}{b - a}$ times the integral of $|f(x)|^2$ over $[a, b]$. Formally, $\\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} |c_n|^2 = \\frac{1}{b - a} \\int_{a}^{b} |f(x)|^2 \\, dx$, where $c_n$ denotes the $n$-th Fourier coefficient of $f$ on $(a, b]$.\n[7] hasSum_sq_fourierCoeff  (theorem)\n    (f : Lp ℂ 2 <| @haarAddCircle T hT) :\n  HasSum (fun i => ‖fourierCoeff f i‖ ^ 2) (∫ t : AddCircle T, ‖f t‖ ^ 2 ∂haarAddCircle)\n    For any function $ f $ in the $ L^2 $ space on the additive circle $ \\mathbb{R}/T\\mathbb{Z} $ with respect to the Haar measure $ \\mu $, the sum of the squares of the norms of its Fourier coefficients converges to the integral of the square of the norm of $ f $, i.e., $ \\sum_{i \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\|\\widehat{f}(i)\\|^2 = \\int_{t \\in \\mathbb{R}/T\\mathbb{Z}} \\|f(t)\\|^2 \\, d\\mu(t) $.\n[8] tsum_sq_fourierCoeff  (theorem)\n    (f : Lp ℂ 2 <| @haarAddCircle T hT) : ∑' i : ℤ, ‖fourierCoeff f i‖ ^ 2 = ∫ t : AddCircle T, ‖f t‖ ^ 2 ∂haarAddCircle\n    For any function $ f $ in the $ L^2 $ space on the additive circle $ \\mathbb{R}/(T\\mathbb{Z}) $ with respect to the Haar measure $ \\mu $, the sum of the squares of the norms of its Fourier coefficients equals the integral of the square of the norm of $ f $. Specifically, $ \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\|\\hat{f}(n)\\|^2 = \\int_{t \\in \\mathbb{R}/(T\\mathbb{Z})} \\|f(t)\\|^2 \\, d\\mu(t) $, where $ \\hat{f}(n) $ denotes the $ n $-th Fourier coefficient of $ f $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I understand the key insight from the Lean search results. The theorem `Polynomial.coeff_mul_mirror` states that for a polynomial `p`, the coefficient of `X^(deg(p) + tdeg(p))` in `p * p.mirror` equals the sum of squares of all coefficients of `p`.\n\nLet me also check how to define Γ properly and look for more details on polynomial operations:\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\"