to its natural degree and scales the result by $ X $ raised to the minimum exponent of its non-zero coefficients (with $ 0 $ for the zero polynomial).\n[2] Polynomial.reverse  (definition)\n    (f : R[X]) : R[X]\n    The function maps a polynomial $ f $ to the polynomial obtained by reversing its coefficients up to its natural degree, leaving coefficients beyond that degree unchanged.\n[3] Polynomial.reflect  (definition)\n    (N : ℕ) : R[X] → R[X]\n    Given a polynomial $ f $ and a natural number $ N $, this function returns the polynomial obtained by reversing the coefficients of $ f $ for degrees $ \\leq N $ using the index reversal map $ \\text{revAt}(N) $, which sends $ i \\leq N $ to $ N - i $, while leaving coefficients at degrees $ > N $ unchanged.\n[4] Polynomial.mirror_X  (theorem)\n    : X.mirror = (X : R[X])\n    The mirror operation applied to the indeterminate $ X $ in the polynomial ring $ R[X] $ yields $ X $ itself, i.e., $ \\text{mirror}(X) = X $. This follows because reversing the coefficients of $ X $ (which has a single non-zero coefficient at degree 1) and scaling by $ X^{\\text{trailing degree}} = X^1 $ leaves the polynomial unchanged.\n[5] Polynomial.mirror_mirror  (theorem)\n    : p.mirror.mirror = p\n    For any polynomial $ p $, applying the mirror operation twice results in the original polynomial $ p $, i.e., $ \\text{mirror}(\\text{mirror}(p)) = p $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Polynomial.sum function coefficients\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 3 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"polynomial coefficients sum of squares\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Polynomial.coeff_mul_mirror  (theore