dot \\operatorname{mirror}(p) $ is equal to the sum of the squares of all coefficients of $ p $, where $ \\deg(p) $ denotes the natural degree of $ p $ and $ \\operatorname{tdeg}(p) $ denotes its trailing degree.\n[2] Polynomial.mirror  (definition)\n    {R : Type u_1} → [inst : Semiring R] → Polynomial R → Polynomial R\n    The mirror of a polynomial $ p $ is defined as the reversed polynomial multiplied by $ X $ raised to the trailing degree of $ p $. This operation reverses the coefficients of $ p $ up to its natural degree and scales the result by $ X $ raised to the minimum exponent of its non-zero coefficients (with $ 0 $ for the zero polynomial).\n[3] Polynomial.coeff_mirror  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : p.mirror.coeff n = p.coeff (revAt (p.natDegree + p.natTrailingDegree) n)\n    For any natural number $ n $, the coefficient of the mirror polynomial $ p^ ext{mirror} $ at $ n $ is equal to the coefficient of $ p $ at the index obtained by reversing $ n $ up to $ \\deg(p) + \\text{tdeg}(p) $, where $ \\deg(p) $ is the natural degree of $ p $ and $ \\text{tdeg}(p) $ is its trailing degree. The reversal operation $ \\text{revAt}(N, n) $ maps $ n $ to $ N - n $ if $ n \\leq N $, and to $ n $ otherwise.\n[4] Poly.sumsq  (definition)\n    : List (Poly α) → Poly α\n    The function takes a list of integer polynomials in variables indexed by $\\alpha$ and returns the sum of their squares. For an empty list, it returns the zero polynomial. For a non-empty list $[p, ps]$, it computes $p \\cdot p + \\text{sumsq}(ps)$, where $\\cdot$ denotes polynomial multiplication.\n[5] Polynomial.mirror_monomial  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) (a : R) : (monomial n a).mirror = monomial n a\n    For any natural number $n$ and element $a$ in a semiring $R$, the mirror operation applied to the monomial polynomial $aX^n$ yields the same monomial, i.e., $\\text{mirror}(aX^n) = aX^n$.\n[6] Polynomial.coeff_reflect  (theorem)\n    (N : ℕ) (f : R[X]) (i : ℕ) : coeff (reflect N f) i = f.coeff (revAt N i)